  "query_preview": "..Little change from the previous discussion. A ridge\nof high pressure building to our west will peak Friday, though its\ninfluence will help support a dry pattern through the weekend and\ninto early next week, even as weak shortwave troughs ride along\nthe northern edge of the ridge Saturday and Monday. Daytime highs\nwill warm to 10-15 degrees above normal for many through Friday,\nwith an exception for the valleys east of the Mogollon Rim where \nlow-level inversions will keep temperatures near to just slightly\nabove normal. Winds are also expected to be light through Friday.\nFor Saturday through Monday, the series of shortwave troughs will\nlead to a slight cooling trend from west to east, with daytime \ntemperatures closer to 5-10 degrees above normal. Winds on \nSaturday and Monday will become westerly and strengthen to 15-20 \nmph in the valleys and around 20-25 mph in the higher terrain, \nwith gusts to 25-35 mph possible, especially over and just\ndownwind of the higher terrain. Localized gusts near 40 mph will \nbe possible near and just north of the White Mountains. On Sunday,\nwinds will weaken slightly between the series of troughs and \nbecome more southerly. \n\nTuesday and onward...The ensemble consensus continues to support a \nstronger trough to move across northern Arizona towards the middle\nof next week. Recent trends have precipitation chances increasing\nTuesday afternoon and continuing through Wednesday. There is \npotential for windy conditions, rain, and high elevation snow \nwith this system, but there is still an degree of uncertainty this\nfar out. We have already seen how good ensemble consensus for \nprevious system, when they are 6 days out, start to see subtle \nshifts to the north and south, which so far have ended up with \nnorthern AZ only being clipped by these systems and seeing minor \nimpacts overall. To see if this pattern holds or if this system\nwill directly impact our region, continue monitor future \nforecasts for updated informations and our latest thoughts.",
